    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I’ve got a heifer that just came out of rumenotomy an hour ago. Can you give me a list of what I need to be looking for over the next few days to make sure she doesn't get an infection and what I should be doing with her food? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A heifer recovering from rumenotomy, you say? That's a delicate situation, quite like a gypsy's delicate balance of good fortune and bad luck. You'll want to keep a close eye on her, just as a goblin keeps a close eye on its treasure hoard. \n\nOver the next few days, look for signs of infection such as redness, swelling, or discharge around the surgical site. It's also essential to monitor her appetite, as a decrease in food intake can be a sign of pain or discomfort. You might need to adjust her diet to make it more palatable, like a goblin adding a pinch of magic to its cooking pot.\n\nAs for food, a heifer recovering from surgery should have a bland diet for the first 24-48 hours. You can offer her a mixture of hay, water, and a small amount of electrolyte solution to help replenish lost fluids. After that, you can gradually introduce more nutritious foods, like a goblin introducing a new batch of freshly baked bread to its underground bakery.\n\nHere's a more detailed list of what to look for and do:\n\n- Monitor the surgical site for signs of infection (redness, swelling, discharge)\n- Check her appetite",
